ogygia installs into an applicable SvelteKit app. First, add the parts: the package, the Vite plugin, and the server handle. Then adopt it one route at a time. All other routes stay normal Kit pages.

## Setup

The CLI is the fastest procedure. It installs the package. It registers the Vite plugin before `sveltekit()`. It adds the server handle and the transport. One command does all of this.

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npx ogygia init
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm dlx ogygia init
```

:::

To add markdown content collections at the same time, use `npx ogygia init --markdown`. If your project already exports a `handle` or a `transport`, the CLI merges into them. It puts your `handle` in a sequence and spreads into your `transport`. It does not overwrite them.

### Manual setup

You can add the parts yourself. Install the package. Register the Vite plugin **before** `sveltekit()`. Add the server handle.

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npm i ogygia
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm add ogygia
```

:::

#### vite.config.ts

`ogygia()` must run before `sveltekit()`. The plugin also sets `enforce: 'pre'`. In monorepos, the plugin adds its package root to `server.fs.allow` in Vite. Thus Vite does not block shim and runtime resolves outside the app directory. For each option, see [the API reference](/docs/reference/api).

```ts
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [ogygia(), sveltekit()] // order matters
});
```

#### hooks.server.ts

`ogygia.handle()` serves the signed island endpoint. `render: 'deferred'` and `render: 'live'` use this endpoint. If you have other handles, put them together with `sequence()`. To change the path, use `ogygia.handle({ endpoint: '/my-islands' })`. The default is a clash-safe emoji route.

```ts
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia/server';

export const handle = ogygia.handle();
```

::: warning In production
Server islands and command / form POSTs go through the CSRF protection of Kit. Thus, set `ORIGIN` on your deployment. Without it, Kit rejects those POSTs. This is one env var. You set it one time.
:::

#### TypeScript types

ogygia resolves some build-time virtual modules (`virtual:ogygia/*`). Your editor and `svelte-check` need one reference line to see their types. Add a `src/ogygia.d.ts`:

```ts
/// <reference types="ogygia/types" />
```

That is the full file. Without it, `svelte-check` shows the virtual imports as unresolved. The build operates correctly in each case. `npx ogygia init` writes this file for you.

## Transport (held regions over the wire)

Held regions render inline with no setup. But some held regions **cross the wire**. Examples: a search remote, or a live content source. Those need the `transport` hook. Install it one time in your universal hooks:

```ts title=src/hooks.ts
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia';
export const transport = { ...ogygia.transport };
```

### Markdown, Shiki & mdsvex (optional)

For `.svx` / `.md` [content collections](/docs/content/collections), add the optional peers. Then set markdown on under the **same** `ogygia()` surface. There is no separate plugin:

```bash
pnpm add -D mdsvex shiki
```

```ts title=vite.config.ts
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    ogygia({
      content: { markdown: {} } // {} = defaults: Shiki + heading ids + collected headings
    }),
    sveltekit()
  ]
});
```

Put `extensions` and `preprocess` in **`svelte.config.js`**. Do not put them inline on `sveltekit()`. Kit v2, `svelte-check`, and the Svelte language server read `svelte.config.js`. If you pass them inline, Kit ignores `svelte.config.js`, and `svelte-check` does not see them:

```js title=svelte.config.js
import { vitePreprocess } from '@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte';
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';

export default {
  extensions: ogygia.extensions(), // includes .svelte, adds .svx / .md
  preprocess: [vitePreprocess(), ...ogygia.preprocess()]
};
```

`mdsvex` and `shiki` stay optional peers. `ogygia.preprocess()` is synchronous. When markdown is off, it does not load mdsvex. mdsvex loads at first use. If mdsvex is absent, you get an error with an install hint. `ogygia.extensions()` adds `.svx` / `.md` only when markdown is on. Set themes and headings under `content: { markdown }`. See [content collections](/docs/content/collections).

## Adopt one route at a time

ogygia is incremental. Do not convert the full app. Set islands on one route at a time. All other routes stay normal SvelteKit pages.

::: tip When to use which mode
A route that is almost static: use `csr = false` plus islands. This gives the largest gain. A route that is a full interactive app: keep it `csr = true`. You can still put an island in it. A full site of static-first pages: use all-islands mode. You do not select one mode for the full app.
:::

### One route

Remove the Kit client from a single route and mark its islands. The other routes do not change. For the effect of `csr = false`, and why islands operate without the Kit client, see the [Overview](/docs/start/overview).

```ts title=src/routes/marketing/+page.ts
export const csr = false;
```

### The router is global

The SPA router is on by default, for the full app. There is no component to render. On routes with `csr = true`, the Kit client owns navigation. ogygia does not intercept clicks there. Thus the two operate together. To remove the router, use `ogygia({ router: false })`. See [Router](/docs/app/spa-router).

### Islands on a normal Kit page

An island does not need `csr = false`. Marked imports operate on a `csr = true` Kit page also. The component becomes an island. The remainder of the page hydrates the Kit way.

### All-islands apps

You can also remove the Kit client from the full app. If **each** route has `csr = false`, Kit usually skips the client build. ogygia finds this condition and runs a standalone islands build. Thus your island chunks continue to ship.

You do **not** need a keepalive route. When Kit would skip its client build, the Vite plugin injects a hidden route with no URL. The route exists only during the build. The plugin then removes it. Islands operate with zero placeholder files in your project.

## Do / don't

| Do | Don't |
| --- | --- |
| Start with your most static route that has the most traffic. There, the removal of the Kit client gives the largest gain. | Do not set the full app to `csr = false` on day one. Convert route by route and measure. |
| Register `ogygia()` **before** `sveltekit()`. Order matters, and the plugin sets `enforce: 'pre'`. | Do not add a placeholder `csr = true` page to keep an all-islands build alive. It is not necessary. |
| Keep true app-like routes on `csr = true`. ogygia operates together with them. | Do not forget `ORIGIN` in production. Server-island and form POSTs need it. |
